INT. MRI SUITE - HOSPITAL BASEMENT - NIGHT

A white tube hums at the centre of a cold room. Warning signs plaster the walls. Through a glass window, a control booth glows green.

DR. SAOIRSE KEANE, forties, radiologist, leans on the console. TECH DELPHINE ABERNATHY, twenties, adjusts a dial. On the sliding table inside the machine lies EUSTACE QUINN, seventies, a patient in a gown, hands folded across his chest.

DELPHINE
Mr. Quinn, we're starting. Try to stay still.

EUSTACE (V.O.)
(over intercom, shaky)
I'm still.

SAOIRSE
Good.

The machine thumps. A grey image flickers onto the monitor. Saoirse leans forward. Delphine does the same. Their faces light from below.

DELPHINE
There.

SAOIRSE
I see it.

DELPHINE
That's not a tumour.

SAOIRSE
It is not.

They share a smile of relief. Delphine reaches for the intercom.

DELPHINE
Mr. Quinn, good news--

On the screen the image jolts. The table inside the machine slides forward. Eustace's hand flies up. A metal tray flies across the room and clangs against the tube.

SAOIRSE
His ring!

DELPHINE
Pull the table!

Both women lunge. Delphine slams the emergency button, Saoirse throws her weight on the stop lever. The magnet whines down.

Silence. They rush into the room, drag the table free, and catch Eustace as he sits up, gasping, his wedding ring tugging at his finger.

EUSTACE
(laughing and wincing)
Forty years it's never come off.
